  <div class="content-callout"><strong>A good plugin should travel in both directions.</strong> It should enter an existing system through a public extension point, then leave another extension point behind for the next capability.</div>

  <p>Martty began as a terminal interface for DeepSeek Harness. That description is still true, but it no longer captures the important part. Martty is now a terminal-native ACP client: it can run as a DeepSeek Harness surface, connect to another compatible ACP agent, or join an existing Cordis client composition. Once it is running, themes, commands, panels, UI compositions, and dynamic Client Plugins can enter Martty in turn.</p>

  <p>This did not arrive as a diagram drawn before the implementation. It emerged while we kept moving features out of the Rust main loop and asking the same question at every boundary: who should own this capability, and can it be replaced without changing the terminal core?</p>

  <img src="/social-preview.png" alt="Martty, a terminal-native ACP client" width="1200" height="630" class="my-8 w-full rounded border border-[#202832]" loading="eager" decoding="async" />

  <h2>First, Martty plugs into the agent</h2>

  <p>The recommended DeepSeek Harness path installs Martty into a dedicated profile:</p>

  <pre><code>dsh plugin --profile martty add martty@latest
dsh --profile martty</code></pre>

  <p>Harness remains the Host. It owns the Agent, Session, tools, permissions, persistence, and credentials. Its ACP plugin exposes those capabilities over a standard stream. Martty starts a separate Client process and attaches to that stream; it does not boot a second Harness or copy the Agent runtime into the terminal.</p>

  <p>The standalone entry keeps the same boundary. The Cordis ACP Client can be the root of its own client tree and spawn an agent command, or it can be inserted as a row in a client composition that already owns the stream. Point it at <code>dsh-acp</code> for Harness, or at another ACP-compatible command. The terminal changes its peer, not its architecture.</p>

  <pre><code>npm install --global martty
martty --agent dsh-acp

# Or connect another ACP agent
martty --agent &lt;acp-command&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>This is the first direction of extensibility: Martty can enter someone else's system without asking that system to adopt Martty's internal model.</p>

  <h2>The Web client model, moved into a terminal</h2>

  <p>DeepSeek Harness Web provided the architectural clue. Its Node Host and browser Client are separate Cordis trees. The Host owns backend resources; the browser owns React, page slots, themes, and interaction state. A two-sided package can cover both processes, but it still creates two plugin instances with separate Contexts and Fibers.</p>

  <p>Martty applies the same separation to a terminal:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The Host tree owns the Agent and server-side capabilities.</li>
    <li>A Node Cordis Client tree owns terminal-facing services and Client Plugins.</li>
    <li>A Rust/ratatui painter owns the TTY, input, layout, and physical rendering.</li>
    <li>ACP carries sessions, prompts, configuration, authentication, and structured updates between Host and Client.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The trees do not synchronize <code>ctx.*</code>, <code>inject</code>, or Fiber objects. A negotiated Cordis extension may carry inspect results, run requests, serialized UI updates, and opaque correlation IDs, but those messages do not turn two processes into one plugin tree.</p>

  <p>That distinction matters when a session is long-lived. Restarting or refreshing the presentation should not silently become ownership of the Agent, and unloading a Client contribution should not tear down an unrelated Host resource.</p>

  <h2>Rust is the painter, not the product model</h2>

  <p>The early TUI directly understood its welcome screen, status area, plan view, statistics, colors, and session facts. That made each feature easy to add once—and increasingly expensive to extend twice. Every new surface taught the Rust loop another piece of product-specific behavior.</p>

  <p>We gradually moved those capabilities into Client Plugins. Plan and usage became consumers of structured ACP state. The welcome screen became slot contributions. Status became a local command and overlay. Themes became registered palette packages. Rust moved toward a smaller job: validate semantic nodes, calculate terminal layout, process input, and paint.</p>

  <img src="/ui_start.png" alt="Martty terminal start screen" width="2134" height="1648" class="my-8 w-full rounded border border-[#202832]" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

  <p>A Martty plugin contributes <code>TuiNode</code> trees, commands, forms, overlays, or palette tokens. It never receives raw mode, file descriptors, kitty escape sequences, ratatui widgets, absolute coordinates, or the complete screen size. The host keeps physical resources; the plugin describes meaning.</p>

  <p>A narrow semantic API can feel less powerful than direct terminal access. That limitation is deliberate. It lets the renderer adapt to narrow terminals and different capabilities, and it gives every contribution an exit path when its owning Fiber is disposed.</p>

  <h2>Then, other plugins plug into Martty</h2>

  <p>Once Martty stopped being the final leaf in the tree, it could become a small plugin host of its own. The Client tree now exposes several public surfaces:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><code>tuiTheme</code> registers complete dark and light palettes.</li>
    <li><code>tuiSlots</code> accepts semantic content for the welcome screen, right rail, and composer docks.</li>
    <li><code>tuiCommands</code> adds lifecycle-owned local slash commands.</li>
    <li><code>tuiOverlay</code> opens native views, selectors, sliders, and forms.</li>
    <li>Session services expose current ACP configuration, plan, status, and usage without asking plugins to parse raw traffic.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A build monitor can register a right-rail panel. A theme package can replace the complete palette. A status plugin can combine session facts with token and latency statistics. Each contribution declares the Service it needs, registers during <code>apply</code>, and retracts with the same lifecycle that introduced it.</p>

  <p>This is the second direction: themes, commands, panels, and packages can enter Martty without modifying its Rust core.</p>

  <h2>Dynamic does not mean exceptional</h2>

  <p>Creator can inspect the actual Client services, slots, tokens, and schemas before it writes a plugin. Defining code creates a version; running it crosses the trust boundary and creates the live Fiber. The package can then be stopped, updated, rolled back, or saved.</p>

  <p>Dynamic code still goes through the ordinary Cordis Loader. It waits for <code>inject</code>, runs <code>apply</code>, owns effects, and disposes contributions through the same path as an installed package. "Dynamic" changes where the code came from and when it entered the composition. It does not receive a privileged second lifecycle.</p>

  <p>Persistence keeps the ownership boundary explicit. A Client-only package can be saved under <code>$MARTTY_HOME/plugins</code>. A package with a Host half belongs to the Harness profile and should be installed as an ordinary Cordis package:</p>

  <pre><code>dsh plugin --profile martty add &lt;package-or-path&gt;</code></pre>

  <h2>Preset is a user-facing unit, not a synonym for Plugin</h2>

  <p>Plugin is a useful unit for authors, but users often want to choose a complete intent. Martty therefore keeps several composition layers separate.</p>

  <p>An AgentPreset belongs to the Host and determines the Agent's capabilities and configuration. Permission options determine how far the next tool execution may go. Martty reflects both through ACP instead of reimplementing them. A Martty UI Preset belongs to the Client tree and composes structural UI contributions such as <code>welcome.hero</code> and <code>welcome.info</code>. Theme selection is independent again.</p>

  <p>They share the idea of naming a coordinated choice, but not a lifecycle or an owner. Keeping those boundaries separate prevents a terminal skin from quietly becoming an Agent definition.</p>

  <h2>The tradeoff is real</h2>

  <p>More extension points create more room for community work, and more ways to write an uneven plugin. Authors now need to understand Services, Loader rows, Fibers, Host/Client ownership, and failure cleanup. Cordis dependency declarations are intentionally loose: <code>inject</code> names a Service, but the actual provider and complete graph are resolved in the running composition.</p>

  <p>Martty tries to put the complexity where it can be inspected. Plugins receive named Services rather than global objects. UI contributions are schema-validated. Static and dynamic packages share one lifecycle. Host resources stay in the Host, and the TTY stays in Rust. Extensibility is not the absence of constraints; it is a set of constraints that lets independent work compose.</p>

  <h2>Try both directions</h2>

  <p>The phrase "plug in and be plugged into" is easier to understand by using it than by defining it. Start Martty with DeepSeek Harness or another ACP agent. Then switch a UI composition, install a theme, or write a small Client Plugin that contributes a command or panel.</p>

  <p>In the first step, Martty becomes part of someone else's system. In the second, Martty makes room for someone else's capability. A plugin that only consumes extension points remains a leaf. A plugin that exposes the next stable extension point can become the beginning of an ecosystem.</p>

